media: cedrus: pad a scaled capture for the display's chroma fetch

A scaled NV12 capture puts chroma straight after the scaled height:
cedrus_buf_addr() points the scaler at bytesperline * height, and the
format says the same, so a 534-line picture has its chroma at
bytesperline * 534. That is right, and stays. What it lacked is room
for the video plane: kernel 0161 fetches in 16-line blocks and checks
offsets[1] + pitch * ALIGN(h, 16) / 2 against the buffer, and a buffer
sized bytesperline * 534 * 3 / 2 (even page-rounded) is 5 pitch-rows
short at 534 lines.

Pad sizeimage to bytesperline * (h + ALIGN(h, 16) / 2) for scaled
contexts. Heights that are multiples of 16 are unchanged; the decoded
pixels and the chroma offset are unchanged at every height.

The green band seen at 534/536 on 2026-10-05 was not the scaler
ignoring its chroma address (cedrus-scaler-check.py has always read
chroma at bytesperline * height at 478, 362 and 242 lines and scored
clean): the VA driver exported GStreamer's surfaces with chroma at
bytesperline * ALIGN(h, 16), fixed in libva-v4l2_request 0015. That
fix makes the exported buffer this size, which is why this one is
needed with it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -376,7 +376,15 @@
 		 * A 32-byte pitch and even height preserve that alignment.
 		 */
 		pix_fmt->bytesperline = ALIGN(pix_fmt->bytesperline, 32);
-		pix_fmt->sizeimage = pix_fmt->bytesperline * height * 3 / 2;
+		/*
+		 * Chroma starts right after the scaled height, where
+		 * cedrus_buf_addr() points the scaler, but the display plane
+		 * fetches in 16-line blocks: at 534 lines it reads 544/2
+		 * chroma rows, past a buffer sized for 534/2. Pad the tail
+		 * so that fetch stays inside the buffer.
+		 */
+		pix_fmt->sizeimage = pix_fmt->bytesperline *
+				     (height + ALIGN(height, 16) / 2);
 	}
 	if (ctx->current_codec->extra_cap_size)
 		pix_fmt->sizeimage += ctx->current_codec->extra_cap_size(ctx, pix_fmt);
